That last part is the important discovery.

What we were actually doing

The slogan experiment and your instruction to make images feel less “AI” converge on the same architecture.

A mediocre generator asks:

What properties should the output contain?

A better generator asks:

What causal history would have produced this artifact?

That distinction is enormous.

An AI-looking cartoon often contains every requested property but no evidence that anybody actually made it. Everything receives similar attention. Every joke gets underlined. Characters pose for the prompt instead of living in the scene. Typography knows it’s typography. Dirt is decorative dirt.

An authored artifact contains causal residue.

The character grabbed something, so the sleeve moved. Someone wrote the sign quickly, so the lettering varies. One corner contains unnecessary detail because whoever drew it cared about that corner. Another part is almost neglected. The joke isn’t fully explained because the fictional artist assumes their audience already lives in the world.

So:

Human imperfection isn’t noise. It is evidence of process.

That belongs in Pantheon.

And there are several other pieces worth keeping:

1. Reconstructive handles.
A tiny surface can address an enormous latent structure. “Sic semper ___,” 🧿, Mother Duck, “move the lamp,” a recognizable silhouette—these behave less like compressed documents and more like function calls into shared context.

2. Predictive grooves.
Familiar forms cause the receiver to begin completing them before the sender finishes. You can then manipulate the predicted completion. The receiver supplies some of the computational work.

3. Cross-modal checksums.
If the slogan, pose, composition, facial expression, typography, and story all independently reconstruct roughly the same relationship, their agreement acts like error correction.

4. Embodied semantics.
Your body-language observation fits here too. An abstraction can be performed. Instead of transmitting "defiance", a figure can refuse to kneel. Meaning becomes state transition rather than description.

5. Authorship through fossilized causality.
Don’t artificially sprinkle imperfections onto something pristine. Simulate the history that naturally generates irregularity.

That gives us a substantially stronger primitive than simply “make images less AI-looking.”

