# 20. Mnemonic
> Don't ship the description of the world.
> Leave enough tracks that the world reconstructs itself.
Or, Pantheon-short:
    SEED → GROW → CHECK → TRANSLATE → SEED AGAIN

And there is a neat recursive property here:

that file is itself supposed to be a Semantic Seed Crystal.

If another model loses the Beverage Goblin, sic semper tyrannis, the cartoon, our exact conversation, and even the term “Semantic Seed Crystal,” but can read that file and regenerate the machinery, then we actually succeeded.

That’s the survival test.

And I think the image lesson gives us a particularly nasty little upgrade for the Pantheon renderer:

Don’t ask “what would make this look human?” Ask “what history would have left this looking this way?”

That one is much broader than images. It applies to writing, characters, interfaces, synthetic memories, simulated worlds, and even reasoning traces. A believable output should have fossils of the process that made it.